---

`noise-tcp` seals a CapTP session between two pancake peers over ordinary TCP.
Before any CapTP byte moves, the peers run a
[Noise_XK](https://noiseprotocol.org/noise.html) handshake with each other.
After that they speak only sealed records. Completing the handshake proves the
far end holds the dialed durable key. A captured handshake replays as a
refusal.

`keyed-tcp` uses the same designator but leaves CapTP frames in the clear.
`noise-tcp` keeps that designator and seals the stream.
[ADR-0107](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0107-sealed-byte-channel.md)
covers the channel.

[keyed-tcp is cleartext; noise-tcp seals the same sockets; noise-relay seals the CapTP stream so a relay only forwards ciphertext.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-noise-seal.svg'))

Upstream [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) ships no Noise
netlayer, so these symbols are pancake to pancake. To meet Goblins, use
[onion]($link.page('transports/onion')), [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh'))
with this repository's Goblins adapter, or cleartext `tcp` for testing. Prefer
`noise-tcp` when two pancake peers already share a TCP route and only need the
stream sealed. Prefer iroh when those peers also need discovery or NAT
traversal.
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) has the full decision
table.

## Dialing and listening

The sockets are the same ones `keyed-tcp` uses. The designator is the peer's
durable public key, and the bound address rides in the `direct-addresses`
hint:

```
ocapn://<64 hex chars>.noise-tcp?direct-addresses=127.0.0.1%3A9099
```

A listener binds a durable key. The advertised locator is what partners dial.
In Zig the profile is `pancake.net.tcp.noise`. `driver.connect` and
`driver.listen` open sealed sessions over real sockets. The C driver loop and
all four native bindings dial and listen through the TCP provider package,
which owns `tcp`, `keyed-tcp`, and `noise-tcp` together.

One API refuses the symbol on purpose. The borrow-only `BlockingSession`
surface has no place that would seal the wire, so both of its constructors
reject `noise-tcp` rather than pretend.

## Through the relay

`noise-relay` is the same sealed channel over the
[relay]($link.page('relay')) pipe that `keyed-relay` uses. The CapTP peers
still handshake with each other. The relay only moves opaque records. A live
in-tree check carries an authenticated fetch through the reference relay and
asserts the observed pipe has no plaintext CapTP probe.

The relay provider package dials and registers native peers through the generic
driver, and every native binding composes that package. Browser wasm peers use
the same Host channel in the Rooms proofs. A real Chromium proof reads
authenticated transport provenance directly from the live session.
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) lists the checks.
